  "text_sanskrit": "तपाम्यहमहं वर्षं निगृह्णाम्युत्सृजामि च । अमृतं चैव मृत्युश्च सदसच्चाहमर्जुन ॥ ९-१९ ॥",
  "itrans": "tapAmyahamahaM varSha.n nigR^ihNAmyutsR^ijAmi cha . amR^ita.n chaiva mR^ityushcha sadasachchAhamarjuna .. 9-19 ..",
  "translation": "I give heat (from the sun); (from within the clouds) I hold back and send forth rain, (abiding in the organs of sense and supporting life) I am Amrita or immortality, and also death (as destroying beings); I am Sat as well as Asat, O Arjuna. (19.) \nV. Sat: the multitude of gross things. Asat: the subtle causes of all, Or, Sat: perfect in excellence; Asat: without a second who is perfect in excellence. The repetition of 'I' shows that all this has not been said merely in a figurative sense, but what is said is fully meant. (19) \nA. In commenting upon the passage 'I, the Kratu,' &c, it has been said that the Lord alone accepts all sacrifices, etc., but that seems objectionable; for, that being the fact, it must be admitted that the Lord also accepts the sacrifice of Traividyas and there would be no difference in the fruits bestowed upon them and on the Bhâgavatas; for in either case the result depends upon the acceptance of the sacrifice, etc., by the Lord; and His acceptance must be the same in all cases. To remove this objection, Sri Krishna points out that the fruit of the Lord's worship by the Bhâgavatas (devoted to the Lord) is far superior to what is conferred upon the Traividyas for their sacrifices.",
